<Layout title="Privacy and Secrets Policy — Open Document Spec" description="How Open Document Spec handles local files, telemetry, and share-aware document visibility.">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="section-header"><span class="eyebrow">Security and data protection</span><h1>Documents should stay where you put them.</h1><p class="lead">ODS is designed as a local-first, zero-telemetry document graph. Your workspace, source files, and private context are processed on your machine.</p><div class="tag-list"><span class="tag">100% local processing</span><span class="tag">Zero telemetry</span><span class="tag">Share-aware visibility</span><span class="tag">Apache 2.0</span></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="container container-narrow"><div class="grid-2"><article class="card"><h2>Local-first engine</h2><p>Indexing, link repair, schema validation, and graph operations execute locally through the compiled <code>ods</code> binary. ODS does not need to upload your repository to resolve context.</p></article><article class="card"><h2>No phone-home telemetry</h2><p>ODS does not include analytics, tracking beacons, or usage reporting. Workspace paths, command invocations, and document counts remain local.</p></article><article class="card"><h2>Share-aware boundaries</h2><p>Documents can declare <code>share: public | org | private</code>. Private material can be excluded from exports and shared reading lists by policy.</p></article><article class="card"><h2>Inspectable state</h2><p>Workspace indexes live beside your documents and can be reviewed, versioned, or removed with ordinary filesystem and Git tools.</p></article></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-muted"><div class="container container-narrow"><div class="section-header"><span class="eyebrow">Policy details</span><h2>What ODS stores locally</h2></div><div class="markdown-body"><ul><li><strong>Workspace indexes:</strong> generated <code>index.md</code> files stay inside the repository.</li><li><strong>Global configuration:</strong> registered workspaces and preferences are stored in your local ODS configuration directory.</li><li><strong>Daemon state:</strong> <code>ods serve</code> uses local IPC or loopback communication and does not expose a public network listener.</li><li><strong>Secrets:</strong> ODS does not request or transmit credentials. Keep secrets out of committed documents and use <code>share: private</code> for sensitive notes.</li></ul></div></div></section>
</Layout>
